speechtext:
The section, as they amend ed it, is as follows :
No master of any sailing ship,	
on a voyage from any port or place in Canada to any port or place in the British Isles, or in the continent of Europe north of Cape Finis-terre in Spain, not being a port or place within the Mediterranean Sea, and during the voyage while within Canadian jurisdiction, shall place or cause or permit to be placed or to remain upon

And then the Senate amendment comes in : -any space where the deck of such ship is not closed in any cargo of any description to a height exceeding three feet above the deck; or upon any uncovered space upon the upper deck, or in any covered space not included in the cubical contents forming the registered tonnage of such ship,-
(a)	Any square, round, waney or other timber;
(b)	Any more than five spare spars, or store spars made, dressed and finally prepared for use, or not so dressed and prepared ;
(c)	Any cargo of any description to a height exceeding three feet above the deck, in any space where the deck of such ship is not closed in ;
(d)	Any deals, battens or other light wood goods of any description to a height exceeding three feet above the deck, in any space where the deck of such ship is closed in ;
That is the amendment made by the Senate, and I understand that the object of their amendment was to carry out 'the idea contained in the present Bill, hut it does not carry it out In language as plain -as that contained in th'e present Bill. The present Bill reads that no master :
-shall place, or cause or permit to be placed or to remain upon, any uncovered space upon the upper deck or in any covered space not included in the cubical contents forming the registered tonnage of such ship,-
(a)	Any square, round, waney or other timber;
(b)	Any more than five spare spars, or store spars made, dressed and Anally prepared for use, or not so dressed, and prepared;
It is absolutely prohibitive by the present Act ' in any covered space or any uncovered space.' Then,, subsection (c) reads :
(c)	Any cargo of any description to a height exceeding three feet above the deck, in any space where the deck of such ship is not closed in ;
(d)	Any deals, battens or other light wood goods of any description to a height ex-

ceeding three feet above the deck, in any space where the deck of such ship is closed in ;
In respect to closed in spaces the prohibition is confined to deals, battens or other light goods, and not higher than three feet, and in places where the deck is not closed in to cargo of any description, wood or otherwise exceeding three feet above the deck. I have consulted very carefully with the officers of the department who have been construing the law, and I am strongly inclined to leave the section as it has been for many years, and as it is well understood.
